<title>BELGIUM: Belgian wage rises pegged to maximum 6.1 percent.</title>
<headline>Belgian wage rises pegged to maximum 6.1 percent.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-12-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Belgian government said on Tuesday that wages in Belgium could rise a maximum 6.1 percent in the 1997/98 period.</p>
<p>&quot;We have decided on the maximum increase of 6.1 percent for the next two years,&quot; Belgian Deputy Prime Minister Elio Di Rupo told a news conference.</p>
<p>The government was forced to impose its own wage norm after unions and employers organisations failed to reach agreement.</p>
<p>&quot;The new norm will allow us to end in an orderly fashion the period of the wage freeze,&quot; Belgian Prime Minister Jean-Luc Dehaene said.</p>
<p>Belgian wages were frozen de facto from 1994 to 1996.</p>
